{"kind":"propose","text":"Proposal for Hugh's adoption under request a601bafd (#22168, current-basis successor to #20819, the adopted lane and worktree identity outcome of #13177), on current bases at main d1ae6ade. One ratifiable outcome: adopt the reconciled lane and worktree identity design, notes/2026-09-09-lane-identity-reconciled.md at exact 77ce6fe2 (artifact git:sha1:f2706d06911eabb310cc43efa23ab8c70fdf2c79), as the governing design for all four layers in place of the 2026-08-27 text adopted by cb755aa1, with exactly the ten differences listed in the companion note (artifact git:sha1:7d23c8d5dc59751d166bc01116acda47f6532c44) and the six-stage delivery it recommends. The ten differences, each an old-text versus proposed-text pair with its reason in the attached note: (A1) an implementing commit's Rests-On trailer is evidence, not authority; only the kernel's signed envelope trailer is verified byte for byte, so no commit text, local config, ref or path grants signing or filesystem authority; (A2) the per-checkout record is a private JSON file in the checkout's own Git directory written with the existing atomic-file and lock pattern, not git config, which the 2026-08-21 decisions note rejected and which the source documents as executable scope; (A3) when the governing lane settles, commands and authoring boundaries warn, name the settling record and ask, rather than refusing on a local value the actor can unset; (A4) commit assistance is a message template naming the correct Rests-On, with an opt-in narrow hook that never replaces a user hook or invokes untrusted programs, and nothing depends on the hook; (A5) merge and retirement print read-only cleanup advice and name candidates; they never delete trees or refs, because gs merge holds a lock and a compare-and-swap window that filesystem work must not sit inside; (A6) lane refs are refs/gitseq/lanes/\u003cgoverning event hash\u003e/\u003cattempt\u003e, allocated by compare-and-swap, so every recut gets its own ref, where the adopted one-ref-per-request shape could not; (A7) a lane ref answers governing record to tip only; tip to governing record is the bounded reverse association, never the ref; (A8) no body.worktree field is added to any durable kind, the one adopted sub-item dropped, because a path is a location not an identity and would be signed prose; (A9) the validated branch field on requests is the existing target_ref under validBranchRef, and layer 4 adds validated optional branch fields only on promise, report and artifact; (A10) duplicate checkouts at one head are reported by inventory and warned at creation, not refused, because a second checkout at the target's own head is ordinary. Everything else in the adopted design carries unchanged: the four layers, cleanup-candidate identification after merge and non-merge endings, duplicate-checkout and refless-head detection, temporary scratch trees, read-only inventory, refusal before signing or unsafe mutation. Delivery under this outcome: stage children S0 to S5 as the note recommends (S0 the design and the architecture layer 6 and 7 paragraphs at one head; S1 the read-only association; S2 the lane refs; S3 the worktree command, record and template; S4 the validated fields with the fold profile advance, landing alone; S5 the cleanup extensions), each with its own independent review and merge; the parent promise ac01f53a stays open until S5 lands, and no design-only landing claims any layer delivered. Kept outside the outcome as explanation: the twelve pure-reconciliation points in Part B that change no policy. Basis note: the adopted note was never merged to main and the adoption cb755aa1 is live; this proposal rests on it and supersedes its text without retiring it. Adoption authorizes the S0 review and the stage children; I implement nothing before it is ratified.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7d23c8d5dc59751d166bc01116acda47f6532c44, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7